L["fishers"] = """Jesus was standing on the shore of the Sea of Galilee, also called Lake Gennesaret, and the crowd was pressing in on him to hear the word of God. He saw two boats at the water's edge. The fishermen had left them there and were washing their nets. One of the boats belonged to Simon, who is also called Peter. Jesus got into it and asked him to push out a little from shore. Then he sat down in the boat and taught the people from there.

When he had finished speaking, he said to Simon, put out into deep water, and let down the nets for a catch. Simon was a professional fisherman, and Jesus was a carpenter and teacher. Simon had good reasons to say no. Master, he said, we've worked hard all night and haven't caught anything. Fishing was done at night, when the fish came near the surface, and they had just finished a long night with nothing to show for it. But then Simon said, because you say so, I will let down the nets.

When they had done so, they caught such a large number of fish that their nets began to break. They signaled to their partners in the other boat to come and help them. They came and filled both boats so full that they began to sink.

When Simon Peter saw this, he fell at Jesus' knees and said, go away from me, Lord. I am a sinful man. He was overwhelmed. He had been a fisherman his whole life and knew he could not have made this happen. He was in the presence of someone holy, and he felt unworthy. He and all his companions were astonished at the catch of fish they had taken, and so were James and John, the sons of Zebedee, Simon's partners.

Then Jesus said to Simon, don't be afraid. From now on you will fish for people. Jesus didn't send him away. He gave him a new purpose. So they pulled their boats up on shore, left everything and followed him.

Why does this matter? This is the moment these men gave up their careers to follow Jesus. It was a big decision. They left behind boats and nets, and in Peter's case a business, a home and a family. They didn't know where it would lead.

What happened after? Peter became one of the closest followers of Jesus. He was part of the inner three with James and John. He also failed badly. On the night Jesus was arrested, he denied knowing him three times. But Jesus forgave him and restored him, and on the day of Pentecost Peter preached to a crowd, and about three thousand people believed. He had become a fisher of people.

There is also a quiet echo at the end of John's gospel. After the resurrection, some of the disciples had gone back to fishing and caught nothing all night. A man on the shore told them to throw the net on the other side, and they caught a huge number of fish. Peter jumped into the water to get to Jesus.

What does this mean for us today? Sometimes God asks us to do something that doesn't make sense to us. Peter was tired and an expert, yet he said, because you say so. Obedience can open the door to more than we expected. It also shows that Jesus doesn't call people because they are perfect. Peter said he was a sinful man, and Jesus said, don't be afraid.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus calls ordinary people, with all their flaws, into a bigger purpose. And it invites each of us to say, because you say so."""
